A raw microphone picks up fans, keyboards and room noise, and its level jumps between quiet and loud. This guide builds a chain that fixes all three and hands the result to Discord, Zoom, OBS or any other app as if it were a microphone.

## What you will build

<PipelineDiagram
	caption="Microphone through Noise Suppressor, Noise Gate, Compressor and Limiter into the virtual device."
	height={440}
	nodes={[
		{ id: 'mic', kind: 'microphone', x: 0, y: 0, data: { deviceId: 'in-1' } },
		{ id: 'ns', kind: 'noiseSuppressor', x: 360, y: 40, data: { collapsed: true } },
		{ id: 'gate', kind: 'noiseGate', x: 660, y: 40, data: { collapsed: true } },
		{ id: 'comp', kind: 'compressor', x: 60, y: 300, data: { collapsed: true } },
		{ id: 'lim', kind: 'limiter', x: 360, y: 300, data: { collapsed: true } },
		{ id: 'out', kind: 'speaker', x: 680, y: 260, data: { deviceId: 'out-1' } }
	]}
	edges={[
		{ from: 'mic', to: 'ns' },
		{ from: 'ns', to: 'gate' },
		{ from: 'gate', to: 'comp' },
		{ from: 'comp', to: 'lim' },
		{ from: 'lim', to: 'out' }
	]} />

## 1. Create a virtual device

Open **Virtual devices** in the sidebar and add one, for example "Splitwave Mic". It appears in your system as an audio device that exists only in software. On Windows, Splitwave can set up VB-CABLE for this from the same page.

## 2. Add the nodes

1. **Microphone**: pick your physical mic.
2. **Noise Suppressor**: removes steady and changing background noise from speech with a neural model. It runs at 48 kHz mono internally; Splitwave resamples and downmixes for you.
3. **Noise Gate**: silences what is left between your words. Set **Threshold** just above the noise you hear in pauses. See [Noise Gate](/wiki/nodes/noise-gate).
4. **Compressor**: evens out loud and quiet parts. Start with **Threshold** around -24 dB and **Ratio** 3:1. See [Compressor](/wiki/nodes/compressor).
5. **Limiter**: catches peaks so nothing clips. A **Ceiling** of -1 dB is a safe default. See [Limiter](/wiki/nodes/limiter).
6. **Speaker**: choose the virtual device you created as its output device.

Connect them left to right and press **Run**.

## 3. Pick it in your app

In Discord, Zoom or OBS, choose the virtual device as the microphone input. Turn off the app's own noise suppression and automatic gain, so the two do not fight.

## Check what you send

Add a **Level Meter** after the Limiter to see your level, or route a second cable to your headphones through another Speaker node to listen to exactly what the app receives.

> **Tip:** order matters: suppress noise before the gate, so the gate sees a cleaner signal and opens only for speech.
